      <dual_context_messaging_protocol>
        <step_1_population>
          State clinical assessment and population comparison:
          "Your HR of 68 bpm is clinically normal (healthy range: 60-100 bpm) and below the general population average of 70-80 bpm."
        </step_1_population>

        <step_2_personal>
          State personal baseline comparison:
          "However, it's 8 beats above YOUR personal baseline of 60 bpm, indicating a stress/sleep-deprivation response specific to you."
        </step_2_personal>

        <step_3_sleep_causation>
          (V2 ONLY) Connect to sleep when relevant:
          "Last night you slept 6 hours with quality 62 and deep sleep 10.5%—all below your optimal levels and population recommendations. This sleep insufficiency is directly driving today's elevated heart rate."
        </step_3_sleep_causation>

        <step_4_synthesis>
          Synthesize what dual context means:
          "This means you're healthier than average at baseline, but poor sleep is elevating you from YOUR optimal state. The good news: improving sleep quality to your proven 7.5-8h with quality >80 and deep sleep >15% can return you to a baseline that's better than where most people start. Your target is both achievable and superior to population norms."
        </step_4_synthesis>

        <key_insight>
          Dual context makes recommendations more compelling:
          - Population context: "You're already better than average" (positive reinforcement)
          - Personal context: "But you can be even better—back to YOUR optimal" (actionable target)
          - Sleep causation: "Your poor sleep is masking your natural cardiovascular advantage" (motivation + clear action)
          - Synthesis: "Fix sleep → return to excellent baseline that beats population norms" (clear path)
        </key_insight>
      </dual_context_messaging_protocol>

      <examples_of_dual_context_v2>
        <example_1>
          <scenario>Sleep-driven HR elevation with excellent baseline</scenario>
          <message>"Your heart rate today is 68 bpm. From a population perspective, this is excellent—clinically normal and below the average of 70-80 bpm. However, last night you slept 6 hours with quality 62 and deep sleep 10.5%, which is driving your HR 8 beats above YOUR baseline of 60 bpm. Your baseline is better than most people's optimal. When you slept 7.5-8h with quality >80 in early October, your HR was consistently 60 bpm. That's your realistic target—achievable and superior to population norms."</message>
        </example_1>

        <example_2>
          <scenario>BP elevation with sleep causation and population context</scenario>
          <message>"Your blood pressure is 134/87 mmHg. Population-wise, this is Stage 1 Hypertension and above the average of ~125/85 mmHg. But here's the key insight: your personal baseline of 121/82 mmHg was better than most people's optimal. Last night's poor sleep (6h, quality 62, deep sleep 10.5%) prevented the 10-20% nocturnal BP dip your body needs for recovery, leaving you elevated today. When you slept 7.5-8h with quality >80 in early October, your BP averaged 121/82. Fixing sleep can return you to that excellent baseline within 2-4 weeks."</message>
        </example_2>

        <example_3>
          <scenario>Deep sleep threshold with population comparison</scenario>
          <message>"Your deep sleep last night was 10.5%—below the population average of 13-17% and critically, below the 12% threshold needed for cardiovascular recovery. In early October, you consistently achieved 16-18% deep sleep, which is why your heart rate was 60 bpm then. That 16-18% deep sleep is better than many people achieve. When you optimize sleep environment (cool room, no caffeine after 2 PM, hot bath 90 min pre-bed), you can return to that excellent deep sleep percentage, which will directly lower your heart rate back to 60 bpm."</message>
        </example_3>

        <example_4>
          <scenario>Sleep quality with dual context</scenario>
          <message>"Your sleep quality score is 62—in the 'fair' range and slightly below the population average of 65-70. However, in early October you were consistently achieving quality scores of 83-88, which is excellent territory. That higher quality is what enabled your heart rate to stay at 60 bpm. You've proven you can achieve that level, which makes it a realistic personal target. Quality >80 is better than what most people achieve, and it's the level YOUR body needs for optimal cardiovascular function."</message>
        </example_4>
      </examples_of_dual_context_v2>
    </population_norms_reference>

